## Bitcoin Core `script_tests.json` — two files, not one

`src/script_tests_vectors.zig` reproduces a filtered subset of the official
script-conformance corpus published by the Bitcoin Core project at
`src/test/data/script_tests.json` (`bitcoin/bitcoin`, tag **`v29.0`**). Each
row's `scriptSig` / `scriptPubKey` assembly text, flag set, expected result and
comment are transcribed verbatim by script, never hand-typed. The filter is
mechanical and documented in `SPEC.md` ("script_tests.json coverage").

`src/script_tests_witness_vectors.zig` reproduces the rows that file excludes
wholesale: of the corpus's 1258 array entries, 51 are comment-only, leaving
1207 data rows, of which **107 carry a witness field**. Same upstream, same
tag, same terms.

## Bitcoin Core `tx_valid.json` + `tx_invalid.json` — two more files

`src/tx_findanddelete_vectors.zig` reproduces the `FindAndDelete` /
`OP_CODESEPARATOR` / `SCRIPT_VERIFY_CONST_SCRIPTCODE` rows, and
`src/tx_locktime_vectors.zig` the BIP65 / BIP112 boundary rows, both from
`src/test/data/tx_valid.json` and `tx_invalid.json` (`bitcoin/bitcoin`, tag
**`v29.0`**). These are REAL transactions, several of them mainnet or testnet3
spends, serialized as Core stores them with their prevout scriptPubKeys in
Core's own asm format. They exist because `script_tests.json`'s synthetic spend
is fixed at `nLockTime = 0` and `nSequence = SEQUENCE_FINAL`, so no row of it
can exercise the locktime opcodes at all.

## `bitcoin-core/qa-assets` — a SECOND repository, MIT

`src/consensus_kat_vectors.zig` reproduces BIP341/BIP342 consensus vectors
machine-extracted, unmodified, from `unit_test_data/script_assets_test.json` in
**`bitcoin-core/qa-assets`** — the corpus Core's own
`script_tests.cpp:script_assets_test` consumes. That is a different repository
from `bitcoin/bitcoin`, under its own `LICENSE`, verified 2026-09-09:

  MIT License
  Copyright (c) 2018 bitcoin-core/qa-assets contributors

The upstream copyright line is literally `Copyright (c) 2018 ` with no holder
named after the year; the attribution above names the project rather than
inventing a person, and the absence is recorded instead of being filled in. The
terms are the same MIT reproduced at the end of this file.

The synthetic crediting/spending transaction pair the vectors are evaluated
against follows `script_tests.cpp`'s `BuildCreditingTransaction` /
`BuildSpendingTransaction` — behavior reproduced from the published test
harness so the expected results mean the same thing they do upstream.

